# ComposePlatform — the architectural spine [SPEC:CXC-VISION]

> Five patterns recur across ComposePlatform at scales that were never designed together, and the recurrence is the
> point. A node announces itself to the fleet, a namespace announces itself to a bridge, a daughtercard announces
> itself to its host, a widget announces its channels to whatever realizes it — one mechanism, four levels, and a
> design check that falls out of it: a mechanism built for level N must be reusable at N+1. `scope` is the membrane
> that makes the topology recursive, so an internal CAN trunk is a LOCALITY rather than a second protocol. One
> semantic value has many physical realizations, which generalises `DP-CIO-6` from transport to realization. Classic
> CAN's eight data bytes force fingerprints on the wire and a registry off it — and force the registry to be
> discoverable, or the wire problem becomes a configuration problem. And underneath all of it: machine signals in
> this fleet do not merely go absent, they routinely AGREE WITH THE WRONG ANSWER, so the epistemics pillar is not a
> postscript but the thing that decides whether any of the other four are known to work.

- what-this-is: ComposePlatform's architectural spine — the through-line that individual specs each express at ONE level. It is NOT a spec: nothing here is implementable, no builder builds from it, and it adds no requirement to any document that cites it.
- why-a-spine-and-not-a-coincidence: The value is the RECURRENCE. Announce-then-bind at four scales, scope as a membrane at two, one semantic value across three realizations — a pattern that holds at four levels is a spine; a pattern observed once is an anecdote. Each pillar below states its levels explicitly so the recurrence claim is checkable rather than asserted.
- synthesised: 2026-08-13, from the 2026-08-12/13 hardware bring-up and bus session.
- evidence-base: THREE sources, and the reader should weigh them differently: (1) one session of hardware bring-up (`docs/board-bringup/`, `docs/PLAN-fleet-baseline-and-bus-native.md`, `docs/HANDOFF-2026-08-12-bringup-and-transport.md`) — measured, on real parts; (2) a CAN specialist teardown (`docs/can-binding-teardown-brief.md`) — an outside read of the wire design; (3) the existing corpus. It is a SYNTHESIS of three days, not a decade of operation.
- how-to-use-this: FETCH it, never copy it. The recovery query is [CXC-VISION-RECOVERY]; a static copy in an agent definition is the failure mode that node exists to prevent.
- blast-radius: This document is re-hydrated into TWO agent bootstraps at every session start, so a wrong claim here propagates maximally and is repeated by agents that never read the evidence. Treat an edit here as an edit to both bootstraps.
- placement: UNRATIFIED — see [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]. The file sits in `corpora/platform-specs/` because that is where its neighbours are, not because the placement was ruled.
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-HAL-DECLARE], [SPEC:CXC-CONFIG-BUS], [BP-CAN-ID], [PCB-BOARDS-OVERVIEW], [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT], [CXC-VISION-RECOVERY]

