## 1. The spine

### [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] Announce-then-bind — one mechanism at four scales, and the design check that falls out of it
- claim: A participant ANNOUNCES what it is and what it carries; a counterpart BINDS to that announcement. Nothing hand-configures the pairing. The pattern recurs at FOUR levels of the platform, and the recurrence is what makes it a spine rather than four coincidences.
- level-1-device-to-bus: A NODE announces to the FLEET. Mechanism: `_device/<id>/*` — `heartbeat` (u32 uptime, presence IS the liveness signal), `build` (u32 CRC-32 of env-name + __DATE__ + __TIME__), `transport` (u8 active substrate + whether it came from compiled default or NVS), `scene` (u16 content hash, 0 = none), `health` (u32 packed free-heap KB, reset reason, flags). Each field is its own namespace and fits ONE classic-CAN frame.
- level-2-namespace-to-bridge: A NODE announces its namespace NAMES to the CAN<->MQTT bridge. Mechanism: a chunked name announcement via `config_chunk` over ONE well-known pre-registered root, `_device/announce`. The bridge learns `name->hash` in both directions. One hardcoded entry and everything else discovered — the DNS-root / DHCP-broadcast-address shape.
- level-3-card-to-host: A DAUGHTERCARD announces a capability descriptor to its host MOTHERCARD over the internal link (I2C or UART). The payoff is that the parent speaks ONE protocol forever: a daughtercard needing bespoke parent firmware has bought nothing.
- level-4-widget-to-backend: A WIDGET announces its named output CHANNELS to whatever realizes it. Developed at [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION].
- design-check: A mechanism built for level N MUST be reusable at N+1. If it is not, it was designed too specifically. THREE instances is enough to generalise from and cheap to get wrong in three mutually incompatible ways — which is the actual cost being avoided, not elegance.
- why-it-matters: Every hand-maintained list in this fleet produced a SILENT failure once it went stale. Announcement is the ALTERNATIVE to configuration, and configuration is where the silent failures live.
- evidence-hand-typed-registry: `CAN_BRIDGE_REGISTER` in `deploy/can_mqtt_bridge/composeio-can-bridge.env` carries NINE hand-typed namespace entries for ONE device (`(a device id, not published)`). The bridge drops what it cannot name, and a missing entry reports only an irreversible hash — the diagnostic cannot name what it lost.
- evidence-stale-inventory: THREE of five `device.yaml` records under `compose_projects/` carry `mac: "TBD"` (dual_throttle, office_fan, office_hepa).
- evidence-uncountable-drop: The MQTT->CAN allowlist was enforced at SUBSCRIBE time, so its own drop counter was structurally incapable of incrementing. An instrument reporting zero because it could not reach the code path it was measuring — the epistemics failure and the configuration failure in one object ([CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS]).
- announcement-must-be-periodic: PERIODIC, not on-change. A MISSING publication is itself the fault report. Query cannot provide that: silence from a poll is ambiguous between "absent" and "did not hear." Same argument that made the bridge's table publication periodic at 5 s rather than on-change.
- designed-cadence: Heartbeat every 5 s; the remaining fields every 60 s AND on change. `_device/broadcast/announce = 1` makes every device republish its full set immediately, identically on MQTT, UART and CAN. Ten devices is ~0.7 frames/s on a 500 kbit segment.
- status-of-the-mechanism: DESIGNED, NOT BUILT — and this is the honest limit on the whole pillar. A 25 s census of `composeio/#` on 2026-08-12 found ZERO `_device/*` namespaces; type code 5 `DISCOVERY` is allocated in [BP-TYPE-CODES] and unused. Levels 1 and 2 are proposals with a task list, not running code. Level 3 has no hardware. Only level 4's widget side exists.
- open-point: MQTT Last Will and Testament would give immediate death detection but is MQTT-only. It must be an OPTIMISATION over the missing-heartbeat rule, never the mechanism, or UART and CAN nodes get worse liveness semantics than MQTT ones for no designed reason.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-VISION]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE], [CXC-VISION-COMPACT-WIRE], [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION], [CXC-VISION-EPISTEMICS], [BP-TYPE-CODES], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-ASBUILT]

