### [CXC-VISION-SCOPE-MEMBRANE] Scope is the membrane that makes bus topology recursive — RESOLVED 2026-08-13 by BP-CAN-ID v2, verified in code 2026-09-03
- claim: `scope` (local / global / remote / auto) is the membrane that makes bus topology RECURSIVE. A bus SEGMENT is a locality: `scope: local` stays inside it, `scope: global` crosses. The relay — and therefore the mothercard — IS the membrane.
- ratified: 2026-08-10 by David: a CHILD device is INSIDE the host's locality, so a host and its children are ONE bus participant with internal structure. Locality is the PARTICIPANT, not the process. Recorded at [CXC-SUB-SCOPE].
- ratified-consequence: A relay must respect scope on the PUBLISH path exactly as it does on the subscribe path, or a naive forward-everything relay leaks local chatter onto the shared bus. The relay is NOT the scope boundary; the host's UPSTREAM transport is.
- recursion: An internal CAN trunk between a mothercard and its daughtercards is a LOCALITY, not a separate protocol. Same wire format at both levels, different segments. Nothing new is invented for the internal bus — which is the same economy [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s design check demands.
- current-defect: RESOLVED. `scope` was SILENTLY LOST on every CAN round trip under the v1 [BP-CAN-ID] layout (`ns_hash(16) | type(4) | source_hash(9)` — 29 bits, no scope field), so `message_from_can` reconstructed a default and inbound traffic was reported "global." BP-CAN-ID v2 (commit `dd7e049`, ratified by David 2026-08-13) added a dedicated 2-bit `scope` field: `prio(3) | type(4) | ns_id(16) | src(4) | scope(2) = 29` bits. Verified directly against the code 2026-09-03: `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h` (`bus_can_id_encode`/`bus_can_id_scope_bits`), `python/composeio/transport_can.py` (`encode_arbitration_id`/`decode_arbitration_id`, lines 171-207) and `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` (lines 602-672, importing the codec from `transport_can` rather than reimplementing it) all implement this layout identically — scope now crosses the wire at every site, not just in the spec.
- consequence-of-the-defect: Restoring scope to the wire WAS a PREREQUISITE for the mothercard architecture, not a tidiness fix — the one item on this page that BLOCKED a build rather than informing one. That prerequisite is now MET; see [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-SCOPE-PRECONDITION] / [CXC-DEVICE-BASELINE-PRE-SCOPE-WIRE-VERIFY] for the downstream verdict this resolution feeds.
- defect-is-not-free-to-fix: RESOLVED, no longer entangled. [BP-CAN-ID]'s 29 bits had no spare room, and a second, incompatible layout existed in `python/composeio/transport_can.py` ([BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE], `[4b type][2b scope][16b ns][7b seq]`). The open wire-format decision this required was David's to make, and BP-CAN-ID v2 (`dd7e049`) IS that decision: a redesigned 29-bit layout adopted by all three sites — `cyd/src/bus_can_id.h`, `python/composeio/transport_can.py`, `services/can_mqtt_bridge/bridge.py` — carrying both a `src` field (from the original spec layout) and a `scope` field (from the transport's divergent layout), plus a new `prio` field neither had. [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE] is CLOSED.
- prior-art: `cyd/src/relay_main.cpp` is ALREADY a node holding two transports and forwarding between them (CAN upstream, UART downstream). A mothercard is that node with CAN on BOTH sides. The code SHAPE exists; scope enforcement and daughtercard self-description are what is missing. Host-proven only (`env:native_relay`, 40 checks) — it has never run on hardware.
- duality: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] is how a thing becomes KNOWN; scope is how far that knowledge TRAVELS. They are duals, and neither is sufficient alone: announcement without scope floods the shared bus, scope without announcement gives a correctly-bounded segment nobody can enumerate.
- still-unspecified: `remote`. [BP-CAN-DATA] byte 0 defines four scope values and `remote` presumably means cross-segment; whether a relay may ORIGINATE or forward it is unwritten.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-VISION]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND], [CXC-SUB-SCOPE], [BP-CAN-ID], [BP-CAN-ID-DIVERGENCE], [BP-CAN-DATA], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-RELAY]

