## 2. The payoff and the constraint

### [CXC-VISION-SEMANTIC-REALIZATION] One semantic value, many physical realizations — the widget is semantic, the backend is the realization
- claim: ONE semantic value has MANY physical realizations. `throttle/left` drives an LED bar on a 480x320 ST7796S, a stepper needle on a gauge cluster, and a 7-segment numeral on a TM1637 — SIMULTANEOUSLY, with no device aware of any other. Each subscribes the same namespace; none coordinates.
- the-seam: The WIDGET is SEMANTIC; the BACKEND is the REALIZATION. That is the whole move, and it is a seam the render engine already has.
- abstraction-already-exists: `GaugeWidget` already supports `style: "dial"` — the default, a 270-degree needle dial (`python/composeio/render/widgets/gauge.py`, `python/composeio/render/engine/parser.py`). A stepper needle IS a dial gauge. The abstraction is not missing; only the BACKEND is.
- generalises-dp-cio-6: [DP-CIO-6] says TRANSPORT is an implementation detail. The generalisation: REALIZATION is an implementation detail. Software Defined Hardware daughtercards are the same move at the PCB level — the parent binds NAMED CHANNELS and does not know whether they are steppers, 7-segments or LED strips.
- channels-not-a-value: A widget maps to a SET OF NAMED CHANNELS, not a single value. This is the design point that must be settled first, because it is the one that is expensive later.
- channels-evidence: `rack_triple_gauge` ([PCB-GAUGE3]) drives THREE 12V PWM backlight lines (3x 2N2222) alongside three x27.168 stepper needles (MCP23017 -> 3x L293D). Per-gauge ILLUMINATION is an actuator belonging to a SPECIFIC widget with NO raster counterpart — on a screen, brightness is just pixel colour, so a single-value widget contract has nowhere to put it.
- decide-before-first-backend: Settle the channel-set shape BEFORE the first non-raster backend ships. Retrofitting a second channel means touching every backend, and there is currently exactly one.
- corroboration: (a local path, not published) ([PCB-BOARDS-OVERVIEW], 2026-07-18) derived the SAME two gaps from the PCB side, four weeks earlier and from the opposite direction: stress S3 wants compound peripherals (`gauge = {needle + backlight}` as ONE object) and stress S4 wants a position actuator (`motor/position`, absent from the nine HAL kinds, which list only `motor/{speed,direction}`).
- corroboration-honest-limit: What is independent is the DIRECTION of derivation — widget-side versus board-side — not the observer. Both derivations are this project's own; neither is an outside confirmation. Stated so the corroboration is not read as stronger than it is.
- what-is-not-built: No non-raster backend exists. The claim that `throttle/left` drives three surfaces simultaneously is a DESIGN INTENT with one of the three surfaces implemented; nothing here has been demonstrated end to end.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-VISION]
- see-also: [DP-CIO-6], [PCB-BOARDS-OVERVIEW], [PCB-GAUGE3], [PCB-HAL-STRESS], [SPEC:CXC-HAL-DECLARE], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]

---

### [CXC-VISION-COMPACT-WIRE] The wire carries fingerprints, the registry resolves them — forced by eight bytes, and it does not add bits
- claim: The wire carries FINGERPRINTS; a registry resolves them into names. This is not a preference — it is forced by classic CAN's EIGHT data bytes, and it then shapes every other design on this page.
- forcing-constraint: Every CAN controller in the fleet is classic CAN 2.0B — MCP2515, TJA1050, SN65HVD230, the Waveshare 2-channel HAT, the ESP32 TWAI peripheral, the CH32V203 bxCAN. NONE supports CAN-FD, and the limit is the CONTROLLER, not the transceiver, so re-buying transceivers changes nothing ([CXC-CONFIG-BUS-BUDGET], verified 2026-08-12).
- instance-namespace: A 16-bit CRC-16/CCITT namespace hash on the wire rather than a name.
- instance-build: A u32 CRC-32 build fingerprint rather than a build string. The build system emits a fingerprint->string map, so tooling prints `Aug 12 2026 21:41:02` without the wire ever carrying it — and a reflash campaign becomes machine-checkable: flash, poll, assert the fingerprint CHANGED.
- instance-announcement: Compact PER-FIELD device announcement — five namespaces, each one frame — rather than one status blob.
- discipline-this-buys: ONE encoding on every substrate, with NO transport-specific variant. That is exactly the leak [DP-CIO-6] exists to prevent: a message that works on one transport must produce identical behaviour on every other.
- corollary-and-trap: The registry MUST be DISCOVERABLE, or you have traded a wire problem for a CONFIGURATION problem. That trade is precisely the hand-typed `CAN_BRIDGE_REGISTER` failure, so this pillar is not independent of [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] — it CREATES the need for it.
- honest-limit: Compaction does not add bits. A 16-bit hash has a 2.32% collision probability at the 56 namespaces measured on the production bus, 7.28% at 100 and 39.27% at 256 (exact birthday computation, not the exponential approximation).
- collision-is-not-a-drop: A collision is a receiver CONFIDENTLY DECODING THE WRONG NAMESPACE. There is no checksum on the arbitration id, so nothing catches it — the failure mode is a corrupted control surface, not a missing message.
- what-adaptivity-buys: An announced / adaptive registry converts collisions from SILENT-AND-WRONG into VISIBLE-AND-REFUSED. That is the difference between a corrupted control surface and an alarm. It buys NO headroom whatsoever.
- endpoint: Stop hashing and ALLOCATE ids from a registrar over the same announcement channel. Wire ids need only be unique among namespaces crossing THIS segment, so a registrar can assign them and distribute the mapping — collisions impossible BY CONSTRUCTION rather than merely detectable. The trade is a STATEFUL segment: a node that misses the broadcast cannot decode until the next one, bounded by the publication interval. Not now; recorded so no layout decision forecloses it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-VISION]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND], [DP-CIO-6], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-BUDGET], [CXC-CONFIG-BUS-ASBUILT], [BP-CAN-ID]

